rnment  that excluded his rivals  houston we have a problem ahead why  nasa's road back to the moon has hit a  huge speed bump  a bit of a letdown for hundreds of  thousands of stargazers who either woke  up early or camped out to watch nasa's  artemis-1 rocket blasting off into space  the launch in florida was scrubbed after  multiple problems artemis 1 is an  unmanned mission to the moon and it's  supposed to be the first step for nasa's  return to the lunar surface  and is hoped to fuel a giant leap to  mars our mike armstrong was monitoring  the countdown this morning from the  canadian space agency in san fer quebec  the biggest rocket ever built was on the  launch pad and ready to go it had also  drawn hundreds of thousands of  spectators out in the early hours to  watch the show well the first issue was  weather the launch countdown was delayed  then came this announcement launch  director charlie blackwell thompson has  called a scrub  for today  nasa engineers ran out of time in the  launch window engineers were nervous at  first over a hydrogen spike in a fuel  tank that issue they determined wouldn't  be a problem but then they hit others  one of the four engines in the bottom of  the core stage of the rocket wasn't  cooling properly and there was an issue  with a vent valve on an inner tank the  head of nasa says the artemis rocket is  too complex to take chances  you don't want to light the candle  until it's ready to go  now there was some disappointment in the  crowds that gathered in florida and at  the canadian space agency in quebec but  this scrubbed launch will still give  engineers information that they could  only get by fueling up and almost  launching this is good data getting this  far in the launch sequence allows them  to test the system  this first artemis launch is a test  itself it won't have astronauts on board  but will make the trip all the way to  the moon about 450 000 kilometers  every part of the mission will be  analyzed and art